--- check
const grids = t.rules((sel, r) => /\.grid\s*$/.test(sel) && r.style.gridTemplateColumns);
const auto = grids.find((r) => /auto-(fill|fit)/.test(r.style.gridTemplateColumns));
t.expect(auto, "In your `.grid` rule, use `grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(160px, 1fr));`.");
t.expect(/minmax\(/.test(auto.style.gridTemplateColumns), "Inside `repeat(auto-fill, ...)`, give a column size with `minmax(160px, 1fr)`.");
t.expect(grids.length === 1, "There's still a `.grid` rule with `grid-template-columns` inside a media query. Delete it, or it overrules `auto-fill` on bigger screens.");
const r = t.$$(".grid .card").map((c) => c.getBoundingClientRect());
t.expect(r.filter((x) => Math.abs(x.top - r[0].top) < 2).length >= 2, "The grid shows only one column. Check the brackets: `repeat(auto-fill, minmax(160px, 1fr))`.");
--- learned
`repeat(auto-fill, minmax(160px, 1fr))` makes a grid that picks its own number of columns. Media queries give you exact control; `auto-fill` gives you less code. Both are fine.
--- summary
`repeat(auto-fill, minmax(160px, 1fr))` fits as many columns as there's room for.
